



:: warrior_queen_day_5_1

Steel met steel in the grey half-light, and for three glorious seconds, I was winning. The rapier's point drove toward Aldric's chest   a clean thrust from the terza guard, rising, powered by the hips and the core and the deepening instinct that was beginning, just barely, to replace the conscious effort of five days ago. The blade moved as though it had opinions of its own, as though the steel and the hand and the body and the breath were all in agreement for once   a rare parliament of aligned intent.

Aldric parried. The practice blade deflected the thrust with the contemptuous economy of a man swatting a wasp, but the parry was //late//. A fraction of a second, perhaps a quarter-beat of a hummingbird's wing, but late. The rapier had been //faster// than his response.

I felt the moment register in his grey eyes before his expression sealed it away. Surprise. Not shock   Aldric was beyond shock   but the subtle recalibration of a man adjusting his expectations upward.

<<speech "aldric">>The thrust is improving. The hip rotation is cleaner today. What changed?<</speech>>

I knew what had changed but could not speak it aloud. The breast guard. Worn beneath the training corset, worn beneath the leathers, the boiled leather cups flattening and shielding the breast tissue with a compression that was tighter and more specific than the corset alone had provided. The breasts, immobilised beneath two layers of support   breast guard and corset together   had become, for the first time since they appeared, genuinely //invisible// during movement. No bounce. No shift. No lateral migration during the pivot. No upward surge during the lunge. The tissue existed, ached, grew, but mechanically it was //contained// with a completeness that freed the body to move without the perpetual, low-grade anxiety of //what are they doing now//.

<<speech "mc">>Better support.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Veletia's doing?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Yseldra's. The breast guard she commissioned. Combined with the training corset, it...<</speech>>

I searched for a word that was not //eliminated// and was not //hid// and was not //denied//. A word that acknowledged the flesh without pretending it did not exist.

<<speech "mc">>...integrated. The chest is part of the system now. Not fighting it.<</speech>>

Aldric gave the millimetre-nod   his version of a standing ovation.

<<speech "aldric">>Good. The body should not fight itself. Each part has a purpose. Each part  <</speech>>

He stopped. His grey eyes shifted past my shoulder toward the yard's entrance, and something in his expression changed. Not softened   Aldric's face was structurally incapable of softening   but //adjusted//. A subtle rearrangement of the granite, as though the mountain had noticed an approaching force and decided to take it seriously. I turned.

[[Veletia arrives|warrior_queen_day_5_2]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_2

Mistress Veletia Roswyn entered the Iron Garden the way a sunrise enters a valley   gradually, inevitably, and with an intensity of colour that made everything else look grey by comparison.

She was resplendent. The word existed for women like Veletia   women who wore silk the colour of crushed elderberries at dawn in a training yard and made it look like a deliberate aesthetic choice rather than a catastrophic misjudgment of venue. Her fiery red curls were piled high and pinned with what appeared to be two golden hairpins shaped like miniature swords   a concession, presumably, to the martial setting. She carried a garment over one arm with the reverence a priestess might reserve for a sacred relic.

<<speech "mc">>Veletia. It is before dawn.<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Beauty does not wait for the sun, darling and neither does necessity.<</speech>>

She swept her gaze across the Iron Garden   the cobblestones, the weapons racks, the training dummies, the sweat-stained evidence of combat   with the expression of a woman who had walked into the wrong room at a party and was determined to make it work regardless.

<<speech "veletia">>Sir Aldric.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Mistress Veletia.<</speech>>

The exchange was brief, formal, and loaded with something I had not expected: mutual respect. Aldric's eyes flickered to Veletia for the barest instant, and in that flicker I read acknowledgment. He respected her. Not as a warrior   she could not hold a blade to save her life, and I suspected the sight of genuine blood would send her reaching for her smelling salts   but as a //specialist//. She understood the female body the way he understood combat and in this matter, her expertise superseded his.

<<speech "veletia">>I have brought something.<</speech>>

She unfurled the garment from her arm with a theatrical flourish that would have been excessive at a coronation and was wildly, magnificently inappropriate in a training yard at five in the morning. It was a corset.

Not the training corset I currently wore   the grey, functional, steel-boned garment that Veletia had produced three days ago. This was something //else//. Something that existed in the gap between the corset I had and the armour I needed.

<<speech "mc">>What is that?<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Your salvation, darling.<</speech>>

[[The combat corset|warrior_queen_day_5_3]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_3

She held it up in the pre-dawn light, and despite every instinct of masculine resistance that still lingered in the crumbling architecture of the prince, I looked.

The exterior was reinforced linen, dyed a practical dark grey that would not show dirt or blood   the same colour as the training corset, but the construction was different. Heavier. More structured. Thin steel boning ran vertically through the torso in eight channels, providing rigid support without imprisonment. The boning was not merely cosmetic or postural   each channel was positioned over a specific muscle group: the obliques, the transverse abdominis, the erector spinae, the serratus anterior. The corset was not merely holding the body together. It was //engaging// it   using the boning to activate the core musculature through gentle, persistent compression.

The chest was the revelation.

The cups   and they were truly //cups// now, not the padded panels of the training corset but sculpted, shaped hollows designed to receive and contain   were lined with a material I did not recognise. Not lambskin. Not linen. Something softer, denser, with a faint sheen that caught the light.

<<speech "veletia">>Spider-silk padding. Spun by the weavers in the East Wing from the thread of cave spiders   the large, harmless ones that Edran cultivates in the sub-gardens. Stronger than leather. Softer than cotton and it breathes, darling   it wicks the sweat away from the breast skin and disperses it through the outer fabric, so the tissue remains dry and the chafing that has been plaguing you beneath your current corset ceases entirely.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>How do you know about the chafing?<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Because I have eyes, Your Highness, and because you have been adjusting the underarm lacing with increasing frequency each afternoon, which is a tell as clear as a banner on the rampart. The current corset is functional but crude   it was built in haste. This one was built with //intention//.<</speech>>

She turned it in the light. The waist was cinched   not severely, not the punishing hourglass of court fashion, but enough to engage the core musculature. The boning at the waist would compress the obliques and the transverse abdominis, activating them through every movement, every breath, every stance-hold and lunge and pivot. Not just //supporting// the core. //Training// it. Turning the corset into an instrument that built strength through the act of wearing it.

<<speech "veletia">>The cups are adjustable. See here  <</speech>> 

she indicated a nearly invisible seam along the cup's upper edge 

<<speech "veletia">>  the panel can be released and replaced with a larger one as the breast tissue grows. I have prepared three sizes. The current cup is for your present development. The second is for where Thalara projects you will be in six weeks. The third...<</speech>>

She paused. A smile played at her lips   warm, knowing, and entirely without cruelty.

<<speech "veletia">>The third is for the queen.<</speech>>

Aldric cleared his throat. The sound was the tectonic equivalent of a mountain coughing.

<<speech "aldric">>Put it on. I want to see you move in it.<</speech>>

[[The fitting|warrior_queen_day_5_4]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_4

Veletia laced me into the combat corset in the corner of the Iron Garden, behind the weapons rack, with Aldric standing a respectful fifteen paces away with his arms folded and his gaze fixed on the middle distance with the determined intensity of a man who would rather be anywhere else during this particular procedure.

I stripped the old training corset first. The leather chest piece came off, then the lacing, then the damp grey linen falling away from skin that was flushed and warm from the morning's drill. Beneath, the breast guard   Yseldra's contribution   sat against the bare flesh, the boiled leather cups dark with sweat.

<<speech "veletia">>Remove the breast guard as well. The combat corset will incorporate its function   the cups are deep enough and rigid enough to provide string deflection. You will not need both layers.<</speech>>

I unbuckled the breast guard. It came away from the skin with a soft //peel//, the lamb's wool lining releasing the flesh beneath. The breasts   free of both corset and guard   settled against my ribcage with a softness that still startled me. The morning air found them immediately. The nipples puckered, hardened, the areolae tightening into dark, crinkled circles that contracted against the cold. Goosebumps chased each other across the mounds in concentric waves, the skin responding to temperature with an involuntary, visible, unmistakably //feminine// cascade of reaction.

I stood half-naked in the Iron Garden. My chest bare, the breasts exposed to the grey dawn and the cold air and the cobblestones and the distant rampart where soldiers paced. The tissue was small   still, still small, barely filling a cupped hand   but //present//. Rounded. Defined. The inframammary fold that Veletia had measured was deeper now than it had been three days ago, the crease marking the point where breast separated from ribcage, the beginning of //hang// rather than //sit//.

<<speech "veletia">>Lift your arms.<</speech>>

I lifted my arms. Veletia wrapped the combat corset around my torso from behind. The linen was cool against my warm skin   a shock that made the tissue tighten further, the nipples hardening to their maximum erection, two stiff peaks that met the spider-silk lining of the cups as Veletia settled the chest panel into position.

The cups //received// the breasts.

The spider-silk was extraordinary. The material cradled the tissue with a softness that was paradoxically supportive   it gave where the breast pushed, but held where the breast pulled, distributing the compression evenly across the entire surface rather than concentrating it at pressure points the way the old corset had. The nipples, cushioned by the silk's depth, softened from their cold-hardened state into a more relaxed erection   still present, still prominent, but no longer //grinding// against an unyielding surface.

<<speech "veletia">>Now the lacing.<</speech>>

Her hands moved quickly. The combat corset's lacing ran up the back in a double-cross pattern   leather ties threaded through reinforced eyelets, each crossing pulling the linen snug against the torso. Veletia tightened in stages   lower back first, then waist, then ribcage, then the band beneath the bust   each stage drawing the corset incrementally tighter, each tightening engaging a new layer of muscle, a new section of the core.

The waist. The cinching compressed the obliques and the transverse abdominis   the deep core muscles that Aldric had been trying to awaken through the feminine guard. The boning pressed against these muscles through the linen, not painfully but //insistently//, as though politely informing them that their participation was expected at all times, not merely during active stance-holds.

The ribcage. The boning aligned the ribs, correcting the slight lateral shift that Thalara's potions were producing as the bone structure reorganised. The compression stabilised the ribcage into the position it would occupy once the transformation was complete   narrower above, wider below, the geometry of a female thorax.

The bust. The final tightening secured the cups against the breast tissue, the spider-silk lining conforming to the exact topography of the mounds   every curve, every bump, every contour of the growing flesh mapped by the material and held in place with a precision that was simultaneously mechanical and //intimate//.

The corset settled. Veletia tied the lacing and stepped back.

[[The transformation|warrior_queen_day_5_5]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_5

Something fundamental //changed//. I breathed   and the breath came from the belly, automatically, because the corset's construction made chest-breathing structurally inefficient. The boning along the ribcage prevented the full expansion that chest-breathing required, funnelling the breath downward into the abdomen, into the core, into the deep place where Aldric had spent two days trying to teach me to breathe.

The corset taught it in three seconds. I stood   and the posture was //different//. Not the prince's broad-shouldered, weight-forward stance. Not the uncertain, wobbling attempt at the feminine guard that had characterised my first days. Something //else//. The boning supported the spine. The waist compression engaged the core. The cup compression stabilised the breast weight. The entire torso became a unified structure   ribcage, waist, hips, breast all held in alignment, each element supporting the others, the whole greater than the sum of its aching, growing parts.

I took a step. Then another. Then a cross-step   left foot over right, hips turning   and the difference was //staggering//.

The wobble was gone. The perpetual, low-grade instability that had plagued every movement since the transformation began   the swimming centre of gravity, the shifting breast weight, the hips that pulled the rotation off-axis, the shoulders that no longer drove the stroke   //stabilised//. The corset held the torso rigid through the transition. The cups kept the breasts from shifting during the cross-step. The boning kept the core engaged through the pivot. The waist cinching kept the obliques firing through the rotation.

I felt //rooted//. Not rigid   the corset moved with me, the linen flexing, the boning bending slightly at the engineered flex-points, the cups adjusting as the tissue within them responded to the movement   but //anchored//. The body had a centre again. Not the prince's centre   that had been high, in the chest and shoulders. This centre was //low//. In the hips. In the pelvis. In the deep architecture of bone and muscle that the transformation was building and the corset was now supporting.

<<speech "mc">>Oh.<</speech>>

The word came out soft. Breathless. Barely a word at all   more an exhalation of surprise. Veletia's smile was insufferable in its vindication.

<<speech "veletia">>Oh, she says. As though the women of the world have not known this for ten thousand years. As though every woman who has ever run, or fought, or ridden a horse, or done anything more strenuous than sitting upon a cushion, has not wrapped her chest since the dawn of binding cloth.<</speech>>

I was too astonished to argue. Aldric had approached. He stood ten paces away, studying me with the evaluative intensity he usually reserved for weapons and fortifications. His grey eyes tracked from my feet to my hips to my waist to the corset-supported chest, reading the posture, reading the alignment, reading the //potential//.

<<speech "aldric">>Assume the feminine guard.<</speech>>

I assumed the feminine guard. Feet together. Weight in the hips. Core engaged. Spine straight. Breath in the belly.

The stance was //immediate//. Not a struggle, not a negotiation with rebellious muscles and uncertain balance. The corset's compression pre-loaded the core engagement. The bust support eliminated the forward-pulling chest weight. The hip width   wider now, wider each day   provided the base, and the corset's waist boning connected the base to the upper body in a continuous chain of supported structure.

I held the stance. No trembling. No wobble. The legs were strong beneath me, the hips stable, the core a pillar of quiet tension that ran from pelvis to ribcage without interruption.

Aldric's eyebrows rose by one millimetre. From him, that was the equivalent of falling to his knees and weeping with astonishment.

[[The footwork drills begin|warrior_queen_day_5_6]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_6

<<speech "aldric">>Today we learn the feet. The stance is the foundation. The footwork is the house built upon it. A foundation without a house is a slab of stone. Useful only to the lichen.<</speech>>

He assumed a position across from me. Practice blade drawn. Eyes steady.

<<speech "aldric">>Three movements. The cross-step, the pivot-retreat, and the drop-step. Learn them and the rapier becomes a hunting hawk   able to strike from any angle, any distance, any position. Fail to learn them and the rapier becomes a garden stake   planted, immobile, and waiting to be yanked from the ground.<</speech>>

He demonstrated the first. The //cross-step//. Leading foot crosses in front of the trailing foot. Hips turn. Body presents a narrow profile. Ground is covered laterally   not a shuffle, not a sidestep, but a //crossing// that wound the hips like a coiled spring and released them in the opposite direction.

<<speech "aldric">>The cross-step uses the hip width for stability. The wider the hips, the broader the base from which the pivot occurs. Your old lateral movement was a shuffle   flat-footed, plodding, designed for a body with its weight in the thighs. The cross-step moves the weight //through// the hips. Your hips  <</speech>>

He paused. Let the observation complete itself without his assistance.

<<speech "aldric">>  are becoming an asset. Use them.<</speech>>

I cross-stepped. Left foot over right. The hips turned   and the //turn// was different than any rotation I had performed as the prince. The wider pelvis generated more rotational momentum. The femurs, angled slightly inward by the pelvis's reshaping, crossed more naturally. The trailing foot cleared the leading foot without catching, without stumbling, without the tangled awkwardness that had characterised my first attempts at lateral movement since the hips began to widen.

The cross-step //worked//. Not perfectly. The timing was rough, the rhythm unpolished, the trailing foot slightly late. But the fundamental mechanics   the hip-driven pivot, the narrow profile, the ground-covering lateral motion   functioned. The body knew what to do. The hips //knew//. They had been building toward this movement for weeks, widening and reshaping and tilting, and now, asked to generate the rotation the cross-step demanded, they responded with an enthusiasm that was almost //eager//.

<<speech "aldric">>Again. Faster and keep the blade pointed at me throughout the transition   the cross-step is an offensive reposition, not a retreat. The enemy should see steel at every moment.<</speech>>

I cross-stepped. Again. Again. The rhythm emerged   step, turn, settle, blade. Step, turn, settle, blade. The corset held the torso stable through each transition, the boning and the cups maintaining alignment while the lower body spiralled beneath.

The second movement. The //pivot-retreat//. Weight drops into the back hip. Front foot lifts and sweeps backward in a crescent. Body spirals away from the attacker's line while the rapier stays extended   a withdrawal that was simultaneously a threat.

I had watched Aldric demonstrate this before. But today, in the combat corset, with the hips that were wider and the centre that was lower and the core that was actively, continuously engaged by the boning's compression  

I pivoted. The back hip //caught// the weight. The front foot swept. The body spiralled   and the rotation was //fast//. Not merely competent, not merely functional, but //fast//. The lower centre of gravity generated rotational momentum that a top-heavy male body could not match. I spun out of the attack line and found myself facing Aldric with the rapier still pointed at his chest, balanced, ready, poised to spring forward.

<<speech "aldric">>Good. The retreat is clean. The blade stayed oriented. Now   the third.<</speech>>

[[The drop-step|warrior_queen_day_5_7]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_7

The //drop-step//. A sharp, explosive descent. Both knees bending deep. Centre of gravity plunging downward   beneath a high strike, beneath a lateral sweep, beneath whatever killing arc the enemy's blade described above the collapsing body and then   eruption. Rising from the crouch into a thrust aimed at belly or groin, the body uncoiling from the earth like a spring released.

This was the movement that would change everything. I did not know this yet. The body did.

<<speech "aldric">>The drop-step is the crown jewel of the feminine combat art. A tall, broad-shouldered man attempting it is slow, awkward, vulnerable in the crouch. His high centre of gravity makes the descent ponderous and the recovery sluggish. But a body with a lower centre   with wider hips for base stability, narrower shoulders for speed, a compact torso supported by the corset and the core  <</speech>>

He paused. Looked at me.

<<speech "aldric">>  folds into the earth like water and springs from it like a blade. Show me.<</speech>>

I dropped. The knees bent. The hips descended. The corset's boning guided the descent   keeping the spine straight, keeping the core engaged, keeping the breast tissue compressed and stable within the cups as the body compressed downward. The movement was fast. Faster than I expected. The lower centre of gravity meant less distance to travel   the body did not have to //fall// as far to reach the crouch, and the wider hips provided a stable base at the bottom of the drop that kept me from toppling.

I was //down//. Crouched, compact, the rapier extended forward. The cobblestones were cold beneath my knees. My thighs burned   the deep muscles that the feminine guard was building, the muscles of the lower body that the transformation was layering with a woman's distribution of strength   but they held.

<<speech "aldric">>Now rise.<</speech>>

I rose. Upward. Explosive. The thighs and the hips drove the ascent   not the shoulders, not the arms, but the //base//, the wide, stable, increasingly powerful foundation of the lower body. The rapier extended as I rose, the blade reaching forward, the thrust driven from the hips through the arm through the steel  

The point stopped one inch from Aldric's breastplate. The Iron Garden was silent. The mist was burning off. The cobblestones were drying. A crow called from the rampart.

My rapier's point rested against his heart. His practice blade hung in the air above the space where my head had been.

He looked down at the blade. He looked at me   crouched half-risen from the drop-step, my hair wild, my cheeks flushed, breath coming hard, the combat corset dark with sweat but holding, //holding//, the rapier an extension of a body that had finally, for one blinding, perfect moment, moved the way it was meant to. The silence lasted three heartbeats.

<<speech "aldric">>Good.<</speech>>

Then, because he was Aldric and praise was a resource more finite than dragonstone in winter:

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

[[Drilling the footwork|warrior_queen_day_5_8]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_8

We drilled until the sun was properly up and the Iron Garden was bathed in golden light that turned the cobblestones from grey to amber.

I stumbled. I tripped. I crossed my own feet and sprawled on the cobblestones with a force that drove the breath from my lungs and sent a sharp ache through both compressed breasts   even the combat corset had limits when the body within it struck solid stone at speed. The tissue protested   a bright, hot flare of compression-pain as the cups flattened against the ribcage, the breast flesh compressing beyond the comfortable range, the nipples grinding against the spider-silk padding.

I rose. Always rose. The corset held. The rapier answered my hand. The feminine guard anchored me. Aldric layered combinations. Cross-step into lunge. Pivot-retreat into riposte. Drop-step into rising thrust. Each combination demanded that the footwork and the bladework operate simultaneously   the feet moving the body while the hand moved the steel, the two systems synchronised by the core that the corset was continuously, relentlessly training.

By midday I was executing the cross-step smoothly enough that Aldric added resistance   slashing at me with the practice blade, forcing me to cross-step //under duress//. The first ten attempts were disasters. I stepped too early, too late, too far, not far enough, and took bruises across the shoulder and two across the ribs for my failures.

The eleventh attempt was clumsy but functional. I cleared the slash and my rapier's point wavered toward Aldric's chest before drifting wide.

The twentieth was clean. Cross-step, turn, thrust, point at his ribs. On the twenty-fifth attempt, the universe shifted on its axis. Aldric swung high. A vicious overhead slash   the killing stroke, Frostfang's signature, delivered with the practice blade but at full speed. The arc was aimed at my skull, and every instinct the prince had ever possessed screamed //block//.

I dropped. The drop-step engaged every muscle the feminine guard had been building. Core firing. Hips bending. Knees descending. The corset held my torso rigid as I folded beneath the arc of the blade. I heard it whistle through the air above my head   parting my hair, close enough to feel the wind but not the steel, the displaced air ruffling the loose strands that had escaped my braid and lifting them like streamers in a gale.

I rose. Exploding upward from the crouch. The rapier extending like a tongue of silver flame. The thrust driven from the hips through the arm through the blade, the corset's boning channelling the core's power into a single, focused line of intent that terminated with the rapier's point pressed against the precise centre of Aldric's breastplate.

We froze. The training yard was silent save for the cry of a hawk somewhere above the castle and the distant clatter of a kitchen servant dropping something metallic.

My rapier's point rested against his heart. His practice blade hung in empty air above the space where my skull had been an instant before.

He looked at the blade. He looked at me. I was crouched half-risen from the drop-step. My hair was wild   the braid entirely defeated, long dark waves falling around a face that was flushed and sweating and shaped by cheekbones that were higher than they had been a week ago. The combat corset was drenched, the grey linen turned charcoal with sweat, the spider-silk cups dark against the chest. Beneath the corset, the breasts heaved with each laboured breath   not visibly, not bouncing, but pressing against the cups with the rhythmic insistence of flesh that was alive and growing and working and //there// and for the second time in my life   the second time in five days   Sir Aldric Vornhelm nodded. The nod.

<<speech "aldric">>Good. Again.<</speech>>

[[Water break   Veletia returns|warrior_queen_day_5_9]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_9

Veletia had not left the Iron Garden. She had retreated to the shaded archway at the yard's entrance, where she had watched the entire session with the focused intensity of a woman observing a hypothesis confirmed. When Aldric called the midday break, she approached with a water jug and a strip of clean linen.

<<speech "veletia">>You dropped beneath a killing stroke and put a blade to his heart. In a corset I built. In //my// corset.<</speech>>

Her eyes were bright. Not with theatrical excitement   not the performative enthusiasm she usually employed   but with something //real//. Something that lived beneath the silk and the curls and the golden hairpins and reached the surface only when her work proved itself true.

<<speech "mc">>It was the footwork and the training and  <</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>It was the corset. Not //merely// the corset   I am not so vain as to claim the garment swings the sword. But the corset provided the infrastructure that made the footwork possible. The core engagement. The breast stabilisation. The postural alignment. Without those, the drop-step is a controlled fall. With them...<</speech>>

She reached forward and pressed her palm flat against the corset's front panel. Against the boning. Against the spider-silk. Against the breast tissue beneath, which I could feel compressing slightly under her hand, the warmth of her palm radiating through the layers.

<<speech "veletia">>...with them, the body is a //machine//. Every component accounted for. Every variable managed. Every ounce of flesh put to work.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Including these.<</speech>>

I looked down at her hand on my chest. At the cups. At the visible shape of the breasts beneath the sweat-darkened linen   a gentle curve, compressed but undeniable, the contour of flesh that was becoming increasingly difficult to disguise even beneath layers of armour and corsetry.

<<speech "veletia">>//Especially// these, darling. The breast weight is not a problem to be eliminated. It is a variable to be managed. The corset manages it. The guard manages it and as the tissue grows   as it //will// grow, enthusiastically, your body has made its intentions regarding breast development abundantly clear   the management will adapt. Larger cups. Different compression. Different materials. The corset will grow with you, Caelan. That is its design.<</speech>>

She removed her hand. Produced a small piece of chalk from a pocket that should not have existed in a garment that sheer.

<<speech "veletia">>Now. Hold still. I need to mark the areas where the fit has shifted during exertion. The left cup has migrated upward by two millimetres   the breast tissue is settling lower as the inframammary fold deepens   and the waist boning needs a quarter-inch adjustment at the oblique to account for the narrowing that has occurred since I took measurements three days ago. Your waist is shrinking, Your Highness. Rapidly.<</speech>>

She chalked marks on the corset's exterior while I stood still, dripping sweat, drinking water, and contemplating the surreal experience of being fitted for a garment whilst standing in a training yard that still bore the scuff marks of a fight I had just won.

[[Afternoon   combined weapons drill|warrior_queen_day_5_10]]

:: warrior_queen_day_5_10

The afternoon brought Yseldra. She arrived at the Iron Garden without announcement   simply //appeared// at the archery position on the yard's east side, bow in hand, as though she had been there all along and the world was merely catching up to her presence. Aldric positioned himself opposite.

<<speech "aldric">>Combined weapons drill. Sword and bow. The first half is mine   rapier and footwork. The second half is hers   archery under fatigue. You will transition between the two without rest. A warrior does not get to choose when the battle shifts from blade to bow. The body must answer both masters with the same readiness.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Elbow high. Anchor at the cheek. If the string finds the breast, I will not be sympathetic.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You have never been sympathetic about anything in your life, Dame Yseldra.<</speech>>

<<speech "yseldra">>Correct. Begin.<</speech>>

Aldric attacked. Practice blade sweeping laterally. I cross-stepped, riposted, was parried, pivot-retreated, lunged, was beaten back, drop-stepped beneath an overhead cut and rose into a thrust that Aldric deflected with his guard.

<<speech "aldric">>Bow. Now.<</speech>>

I sheathed the rapier. Seized the bow from the rack. Nocked an arrow. Drew   elbow high, the feminine draw, the anchor at my cheekbone, the string arcing cleanly above the breast, the combat corset holding the tissue stable as the ribcage expanded with the draw's tension. I aimed. Released. The arrow hit the third ring.

<<speech "yseldra">>Not good enough. Again. Faster.<</speech>>

I nocked. Drew. Released. Second ring.

<<speech "yseldra">>Better. Now sword.<</speech>>

I dropped the bow. Drew the rapier. Aldric was already moving   a thrust aimed at my chest. I parried, cross-stepped, riposted. The exchange lasted twenty seconds. My arms were burning. My shoulder ached from the archery draw. The transition from bow-muscles to sword-muscles was jarring   different grips, different ranges of motion, different demands on shoulders that were narrowing and wrists that were loosening and a chest that was growing.

